/* TrainTanmay Chemistry – AQA 7405 3.2 Inorganic chemistry: topics, notes, key terms, flashcards, equations, quiz questions and PMT packs.
   Notes are AI-written summaries to check against the AQA specification and a textbook. */
(function(){
const S=window.SPEC;
S.addTopic({"id":"3.2.1","mod":2,"as":true,"title":"Periodicity","short":"Classification into blocks and trends across Period 3.","spec":["Classifying elements into s, p, d and f blocks","Trends across Period 3: atomic radius, first ionisation energy and melting point","Explaining these trends in terms of structure and bonding"],"notes":"\n<h2>The periodic table</h2>\n<p>Elements are arranged in order of increasing <strong>atomic number</strong>. Elements in the same <strong>group</strong> (column) have the same outer-shell electron configuration, so they have similar chemical properties. Each <strong>period</strong> (row) is a new shell being filled. Mendeleev arranged elements by properties and left gaps for undiscovered elements, predicting their properties; this is why the table \"works\".</p>\n<p>Elements are also classified by <strong>block</strong>: s, p or d, depending on the sub-shell their highest-energy electron is in (see 2.2.1). <strong>Periodicity</strong> is the repeating pattern of physical and chemical properties across each period.</p>\n\n<h2>Structure and bonding across a period</h2>\n<h3>Metallic bonding</h3>\n<p><strong>Metallic bonding</strong> is the strong electrostatic attraction between positive metal ions (cations) and <strong>delocalised electrons</strong>. The metal is a giant metallic lattice: a regular arrangement of cations in a \"sea\" of electrons that can move through the whole structure.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>High melting points</strong>: strong attraction between cations and delocalised electrons throughout the lattice.</li>\n<li><strong>Good electrical conductors</strong> (solid and liquid): the delocalised electrons can move and carry charge.</li>\n<li><strong>Malleable and ductile</strong>: layers of ions can slide over each other while the delocalised electrons still hold them together.</li>\n<li>Insoluble (except by reacting with water, as Group 1 metals do).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Giant covalent lattices</h3>\n<p>Many atoms joined by strong covalent bonds in a continuous network. Diamond, graphite, graphene (carbon) and silicon.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Diamond</strong> and <strong>silicon</strong>: each atom covalently bonded to four others in a tetrahedral network. Very high melting point (many strong covalent bonds must break), very hard, no free electrons so no conduction (silicon is a semiconductor).</li>\n<li><strong>Graphite</strong>: each carbon bonded to three others in flat hexagonal layers; the fourth electron is delocalised across the layer. Layers are held by weak London forces. Very high melting point; conducts electricity along the layers; soft and slippery because layers slide.</li>\n<li><strong>Graphene</strong>: a single layer of graphite, one atom thick. Very strong, conducts electricity extremely well, transparent and light.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Giant covalent substances are insoluble in all solvents: the covalent bonds are too strong to be broken by solvent interactions.</p>\n<h3>Melting points across periods 2 and 3</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><th>Period 3</th><th>Na</th><th>Mg</th><th>Al</th><th>Si</th><th>P₄</th><th>S₈</th><th>Cl₂</th><th>Ar</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Structure</td><td colspan=\"3\">giant metallic</td><td>giant covalent</td><td colspan=\"4\">simple molecular</td></tr>\n<tr><td>m.p. / °C</td><td>98</td><td>650</td><td>660</td><td>1410</td><td>44</td><td>113</td><td>−101</td><td>−189</td></tr></table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Na → Al</strong>: rises. The ions have increasing charge (+1, +2, +3) and decreasing size, and there are more delocalised electrons per atom, so the metallic bonding gets stronger.</li>\n<li><strong>Si</strong>: highest, because melting means breaking many strong covalent bonds in a giant lattice.</li>\n<li><strong>P₄, S₈, Cl₂, Ar</strong>: low, because only weak London forces between molecules are overcome. S₈ is highest of these because it has the most electrons (strongest London forces), then P₄, Cl₂ and Ar.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Period 2 follows the same pattern: Li and Be (giant metallic), B and C (giant covalent, very high), N₂, O₂, F₂, Ne (simple molecular, very low).</p>\n<blockquote class=\"tip\"><p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> explaining ionisation energy trends always needs the three factors: nuclear charge, distance (atomic radius), shielding. For the dips, name the sub-shell (2p is higher in energy than 2s) or say \"paired electrons in the same orbital repel\".</p></blockquote>\n\n<div class=\"wex\"><p><strong>Worked example.</strong> Explain why the first ionisation energy of sodium is much lower than that of neon.</p>\n<p>Sodium's outer electron is in a <strong>new shell</strong> (3s), further from the nucleus, with more <strong>shielding</strong> from two inner shells. Despite the extra proton, it is held much less strongly than neon's 2p electrons, so less energy is needed to remove it.</p></div>\n<h2>Check yourself</h2><p class=\"muted\">Try each one before opening the answer.</p><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Write the equation for the second ionisation energy of calcium.</summary><div><p>Ca⁺(g) → Ca²⁺(g) + e⁻</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Why does aluminium have a lower first ionisation energy than magnesium?</summary><div><p>Al's outer electron is in a 3p sub-shell, higher in energy (and slightly more shielded) than Mg's 3s, so it is easier to remove.</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Why does graphite conduct electricity but diamond does not?</summary><div><p>In graphite each carbon uses three electrons in bonds; the fourth is delocalised and can move along the layers. In diamond all four are used in covalent bonds.</p></div></details>","terms":[["First ionisation energy","The energy required to remove one electron from each atom in one mole of gaseous atoms of an element to form one mole of gaseous 1+ ions."],["Metallic bonding","Strong electrostatic attraction between cations and delocalised electrons."],["Periodicity","A repeating trend in physical and chemical properties across each period."],["Successive ionisation energy","The energy to remove each electron in turn from one mole of gaseous ions."],["Giant covalent lattice","A network of atoms held together by strong covalent bonds, e.g. diamond, graphite, silicon."]],"qa":[["Three factors affecting ionisation energy","Atomic radius, nuclear charge and electron shielding."],["Trend in first ionisation energy across a period","Generally increases: more protons, similar shielding, smaller radius."],["Trend in first ionisation energy down a group","Decreases: bigger radius and more shielding outweigh the extra nuclear charge."],["Why is the first IE of B lower than Be?","B’s outer electron is in a 2p sub-shell, higher in energy than 2s, so it is easier to remove."],["Why is the first IE of O lower than N?","O’s 2p⁴ has a paired electron; repulsion between the paired electrons makes one easier to remove."],["What do big jumps in successive ionisation energies show?","An electron is being removed from a shell closer to the nucleus: they reveal the group number."],["Trend in melting point across period 3","Rises from Na to Si (metallic then giant covalent), then drops sharply for P₄, S₈, Cl₂, Ar (simple molecular)."],["Why does graphite conduct electricity?","Each C has one delocalised electron that moves between the layers."],["Why is diamond hard with a very high melting point?","Each C is bonded to four others in a giant covalent lattice of strong bonds."],["Which period 2 and 3 elements are giant covalent?","Carbon (diamond, graphite, graphene) and silicon."]],"eqs":[["$\\mathrm{X(g)}\\longrightarrow\\mathrm{X^+(g)}+\\mathrm{e^-}$","First ionisation energy equation"]],"mcq":[{"q":"Which Period 3 element has the highest melting point?","o":["Mg","Al","Si","P"],"a":2,"e":"Silicon is macromolecular: many strong covalent bonds must break."}],"exam":[],"labs":[],"pags":[],"packs":[{"set":"Topic questions","label":"Inorganic Chemistry 1 questions","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Topic-Qs/AQA/Inorganic-I/OUP-Chapterwide/Inorganic%20Chemistry%201%20QP.pdf"},{"set":"Topic questions","label":"Inorganic Chemistry 1 mark scheme","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Topic-Qs/AQA/Inorganic-I/OUP-Chapterwide/Inorganic%20Chemistry%201%20MS.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"PMT notes: Periodicity","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Inorganic-I/Detailed/2.1.%20Periodicity.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Knockhardy notes: Periodicity","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Inorganic-I/Knockhardy/2.1.%20Periodicity.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Mind map: Periodicity","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Inorganic-I/Mind-Maps/2.1.%20Periodicity.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Definitions: Inorganic Chemistry 1","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Inorganic-I/Definitions%20-%20Inorganic%20Chemistry%20I%20-%20AQA%20Chemistry%20A-level.pdf"}]});
S.addTopic({"id":"3.2.2","mod":2,"as":true,"title":"Group 2, the alkaline earth metals","short":"Trends in atomic radius, ionisation energy and melting point; reactions with water; solubilities and uses.","spec":["Trends in atomic radius, first ionisation energy and melting point from Mg to Ba","Reactions of the elements Mg–Ba with water; magnesium burns in steam to give MgO and H₂","Use of magnesium in the extraction of titanium from TiCl₄","Relative solubilities of the hydroxides (more soluble down the group) and sulfates (less soluble down the group)","Uses: Mg(OH)₂ in medicine, Ca(OH)₂ in agriculture, CaO or CaCO₃ to remove SO₂ from flue gases, BaSO₄ in medicine","Testing for sulfate ions with acidified BaCl₂ (required practical 4)"],"notes":"\n<h2>Group 2: the alkaline earth metals</h2>\n<p>Be, Mg, Ca, Sr, Ba. Each has two electrons in its outer s sub-shell (ns²). In reactions they lose those two electrons to form <strong>2+ ions</strong>, so they are <strong>reducing agents</strong>: they are oxidised from 0 to +2.</p>\n<h3>Reactivity increases down the group</h3>\n<p>Going down, atoms get bigger and the outer electrons are more shielded, so the <strong>first and second ionisation energies decrease</strong>. The outer electrons are lost more easily, so the elements react more vigorously.</p>\n\n<h2>Redox reactions</h2>\n<h3>With oxygen</h3>\n<div class=\"rxn\">2Mg(s) + O₂(g) → 2MgO(s)</div>\n<p>Burning magnesium gives a brilliant white flame. Oxidation numbers: Mg 0 → +2 (oxidised), O 0 → −2 (reduced).</p>\n<h3>With water</h3>\n<p>Group 2 metals react with water to form a <strong>hydroxide</strong> and <strong>hydrogen</strong>. Reactivity increases down the group: Mg reacts only very slowly with cold water; Ca, Sr and Ba react increasingly vigorously.</p>\n<div class=\"rxn\">Ca(s) + 2H₂O(l) → Ca(OH)₂(aq) + H₂(g)</div>\n<p>Magnesium reacts with steam to form the oxide: Mg(s) + H₂O(g) → MgO(s) + H₂(g).</p>\n<h3>With dilute acids</h3>\n<div class=\"rxn\">Mg(s) + 2HCl(aq) → MgCl₂(aq) + H₂(g)</div>\n<p>With sulfuric acid, the reaction slows and stops for Ca, Sr and Ba because their sulfates are insoluble and coat the metal.</p>\n\n<h2>Oxides and hydroxides</h2>\n<p>Group 2 oxides react with water to form hydroxides, releasing OH⁻ ions:</p>\n<div class=\"rxn\">CaO(s) + H₂O(l) → Ca(OH)₂(aq) → Ca²⁺(aq) + 2OH⁻(aq)</div>\n<p>The <strong>solubility of the hydroxides increases down the group</strong>: Mg(OH)₂ is only slightly soluble, Ba(OH)₂ dissolves well. So the solutions are <strong>more alkaline</strong> further down: a saturated Mg(OH)₂ solution has pH about 10, Ba(OH)₂ about 13.</p>\n<h2>Uses of Group 2 compounds as bases</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Calcium hydroxide</strong> (slaked lime) is used in agriculture to <strong>neutralise acidic soils</strong>: Ca(OH)₂ + 2H⁺ → Ca²⁺ + 2H₂O.</li>\n<li><strong>Magnesium hydroxide</strong> (milk of magnesia) and <strong>calcium carbonate</strong> are used as <strong>antacids</strong> to neutralise excess stomach acid (HCl):<br>Mg(OH)₂ + 2HCl → MgCl₂ + 2H₂O<br>CaCO₃ + 2HCl → CaCl₂ + H₂O + CO₂</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote class=\"tip\"><p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> when you explain why reactivity increases down Group 2, link it to ionisation energy: atomic radius and shielding increase, so less energy is needed to remove the outer electrons. Then say that the metal loses electrons more easily.</p></blockquote>\n\n<h2>Explaining the trend in detail</h2>\n<p>Going down Group 2, each element has one more shell of electrons. Three effects follow:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The <strong>atomic radius increases</strong>, so the outer electrons are further from the nucleus.</li>\n<li>There is <strong>more shielding</strong> from the extra inner shells.</li>\n<li>The <strong>nuclear charge increases</strong>, but its effect is outweighed by distance and shielding.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>So the attraction between the nucleus and the outer electrons decreases, the first and second ionisation energies decrease, and the metals lose their outer electrons more easily: they become more reactive (stronger reducing agents).</p>\n<h2>Observations to learn</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><th>Element</th><th>With cold water</th><th>Burning in oxygen</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Mg</td><td>very slow; a few bubbles over days</td><td>bright white flame, white solid MgO</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ca</td><td>steady fizzing; cloudy white suspension (Ca(OH)₂ only slightly soluble)</td><td>brick-red flame</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Sr</td><td>faster fizzing</td><td>crimson flame</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ba</td><td>vigorous fizzing; colourless solution</td><td>pale green flame</td></tr></table></div>\n<h2>Writing redox equations for Group 2</h2>\n<div class=\"wex\"><p><strong>Worked example.</strong> Write the equation for strontium with water, and show it is a redox reaction.</p>\n<p>Sr(s) + 2H₂O(l) → Sr(OH)₂(aq) + H₂(g)</p>\n<p>Sr: 0 → +2, oxidised. H: +1 (in H₂O) → 0 (in H₂), reduced. Electrons are transferred from Sr to H.</p></div>\n<div class=\"wex\"><p><strong>Worked example (calculation).</strong> An antacid tablet contains 0.500 g of CaCO₃. What volume of 0.100 mol dm⁻³ HCl can it neutralise?</p>\n$$n(\\mathrm{CaCO_3})=\\dfrac{0.500}{100.1}=5.00\\times 10^{-3}\\ \\text{mol}$$<p>CaCO₃ reacts with 2HCl, so n(HCl) = 1.00 × 10⁻² mol.</p>\n$$V=\\dfrac{n}{c}=\\dfrac{1.00\\times 10^{-2}}{0.100}=0.100\\ \\text{dm}^3=\\mathbf{100}\\ \\text{cm}^3$$</div>\n<h2>Check yourself</h2><p class=\"muted\">Try each one before opening the answer.</p><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Why is barium more reactive than magnesium?</summary><div><p>Barium atoms are larger with more shielding, so the outer electrons are less strongly attracted to the nucleus; the ionisation energies are lower and the electrons are lost more easily.</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Predict the pH of a saturated solution of barium hydroxide compared with magnesium hydroxide.</summary><div><p>Higher (about 13 versus about 10): Ba(OH)₂ is more soluble, so the solution contains more OH⁻ ions.</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Write the equation for calcium oxide reacting with water.</summary><div><p>CaO(s) + H₂O(l) → Ca(OH)₂(aq)</p></div></details>\n<h2>More to try</h2><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Write the equation for magnesium reacting with steam.</summary><div><p>Mg(s) + H₂O(g) → MgO(s) + H₂(g)</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Explain why the second ionisation energy of Mg is larger than the first.</summary><div><p>The second electron is removed from a positive ion (Mg⁺), which attracts it more strongly; the ion is also smaller.</p></div></details>\n<h2>AQA Group 2 details</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Melting point</strong> generally decreases down the group: the metal ions get larger, so the attraction between the delocalised electrons and the cations weakens. (Mg is an anomaly because of its different crystal structure.)</li>\n<li><strong>Magnesium with steam</strong>: Mg(s) + H₂O(g) → MgO(s) + H₂(g), a bright white flame. With cold water Mg reacts only very slowly to give Mg(OH)₂.</li>\n<li><strong>Extracting titanium</strong>: TiO₂ is converted to TiCl₄, which is reduced by magnesium: TiCl₄ + 2Mg → Ti + 2MgCl₂ (in an inert argon atmosphere at high temperature).</li>\n<li><strong>Solubility</strong>: hydroxides become more soluble down the group (Mg(OH)₂ is sparingly soluble); sulfates become less soluble (BaSO₄ is insoluble).</li>\n<li><strong>Uses</strong>: Mg(OH)₂ is an antacid (milk of magnesia); Ca(OH)₂ neutralises acidic soil; CaO or CaCO₃ removes SO₂ from flue gases (CaO + SO₂ → CaSO₃); BaSO₄ is a “barium meal” for X-rays, safe because it is insoluble even though Ba²⁺ ions are toxic.</li>\n<li><strong>Sulfate test</strong>: acidify with HCl (to remove carbonate), then add BaCl₂ (aq): a white precipitate means sulfate.</li>\n</ul>\n","terms":[["Group 2 elements","The alkaline earth metals; they lose two electrons to form 2+ ions."]],"qa":[["Group 2 metal + water →","Metal hydroxide + hydrogen, e.g. Ca + 2H₂O → Ca(OH)₂ + H₂."],["Group 2 metal + oxygen →","Metal oxide, e.g. 2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO."],["Group 2 metal + dilute acid →","Salt + hydrogen."],["Group 2 oxide + water →","Hydroxide (alkaline solution), e.g. CaO + H₂O → Ca(OH)₂; pH about 10–12."],["Trend in reactivity down group 2","Increases: ionisation energies decrease, so electrons are lost more easily."],["Trend in solubility of group 2 hydroxides","More soluble down the group, so solutions are more alkaline."],["Use of Ca(OH)₂ in farming","Slaked lime neutralises acidic soil."],["Use of Mg(OH)₂ and CaCO₃ in medicine","Antacids to neutralise excess stomach acid."],["Is group 2 + water a redox reaction?","Yes: the metal is oxidised (0 → +2), hydrogen is reduced (+1 → 0)."],["What forms when magnesium burns in steam?","Magnesium oxide and hydrogen: Mg + H₂O → MgO + H₂."],["How is magnesium used to extract titanium?","It reduces TiCl₄: TiCl₄ + 2Mg → Ti + 2MgCl₂."],["Why is BaSO₄ safe to swallow for X-rays when Ba²⁺ is toxic?","BaSO₄ is insoluble, so the toxic Ba²⁺ ions are not absorbed."],["How is SO₂ removed from flue gases?","With CaO or CaCO₃: CaO + SO₂ → CaSO₃."]],"eqs":[],"mcq":[{"q":"Which Group 2 hydroxide is least soluble in water?","o":["Mg(OH)₂","Ca(OH)₂","Sr(OH)₂","Ba(OH)₂"],"a":0,"e":"Hydroxides get more soluble down the group, so Mg(OH)₂ is the least soluble."}],"exam":[],"labs":[],"pags":[],"packs":[{"set":"Topic questions","label":"Inorganic Chemistry 1 questions","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Topic-Qs/AQA/Inorganic-I/OUP-Chapterwide/Inorganic%20Chemistry%201%20QP.pdf"},{"set":"Topic questions","label":"Inorganic Chemistry 1 mark scheme","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Topic-Qs/AQA/Inorganic-I/OUP-Chapterwide/Inorganic%20Chemistry%201%20MS.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"PMT notes: Group 2 Metals","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Inorganic-I/Detailed/2.2.%20Group%202%20Metals.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Knockhardy notes: Group 2 - Trends, Reactions and Properties","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Inorganic-I/Knockhardy/2.2.%20Group%202%20-%20Trends,%20Reactions%20and%20Properties.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Mind map: Group 2, the Alkaline Earth Metals","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Inorganic-I/Mind-Maps/2.2.%20Group%202,%20the%20Alkaline%20Earth%20Metals.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Definitions: Inorganic Chemistry 1","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Inorganic-I/Definitions%20-%20Inorganic%20Chemistry%20I%20-%20AQA%20Chemistry%20A-level.pdf"}]});
S.addTopic({"id":"3.2.3","mod":2,"as":true,"title":"Group 7(17), the halogens","short":"Halogen trends, displacement, halide ions as reducing agents, tests for ions, and chlorine in water.","spec":["Trends in electronegativity and boiling point of the halogens","Oxidising ability of the halogens decreases down the group: displacement reactions","Reducing ability of halide ions increases down the group: reactions of solid sodium halides with concentrated sulfuric acid","Identifying halide ions with acidified silver nitrate, and the solubility of the silver halides in ammonia","Reactions of chlorine with water and cold dilute NaOH; uses in water treatment and bleach","Required practical 4: tests for Group 2 cations, ammonium, hydroxide, carbonate, sulfate and halide ions"],"notes":"\n<h2>Group 7: the halogens</h2>\n<p>F₂, Cl₂, Br₂, I₂ exist as <strong>diatomic molecules</strong>. Each atom has seven outer electrons (ns² np⁵) and needs one more, so the halogens are <strong>oxidising agents</strong>: they gain one electron to form 1− halide ions, and are themselves reduced.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><th>Halogen</th><th>State at RTP</th><th>Colour of element</th><th>In water</th><th>In cyclohexane</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Cl₂</td><td>gas</td><td>pale green</td><td>pale green</td><td>pale green</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Br₂</td><td>liquid</td><td>red-brown</td><td>orange</td><td>orange</td></tr>\n<tr><td>I₂</td><td>solid</td><td>shiny grey-black</td><td>brown</td><td>violet</td></tr></table></div>\n<h3>Boiling points increase down the group</h3>\n<p>Down the group the molecules have <strong>more electrons</strong>, so the <strong>London forces</strong> between molecules are stronger, and more energy is needed to overcome them. That's why the state changes from gas to liquid to solid.</p>\n\n<h2>Reactivity decreases down the group</h2>\n<p>Down the group, atomic radius and shielding increase, so the nucleus attracts an incoming electron less strongly. The halogen gains an electron less easily: <strong>oxidising power decreases</strong> down the group.</p>\n<h3>Displacement reactions</h3>\n<p>A more reactive halogen oxidises the halide ions of a less reactive halogen, displacing it. Add a few drops of the halogen solution to the halide solution; then add cyclohexane and shake to see the colour more clearly in the organic (top) layer.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><th>Add →</th><th>KCl(aq)</th><th>KBr(aq)</th><th>KI(aq)</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Cl₂(aq)</td><td>no reaction</td><td>orange (Br₂)</td><td>brown; violet in cyclohexane (I₂)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Br₂(aq)</td><td>no reaction</td><td>no reaction</td><td>brown; violet in cyclohexane (I₂)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>I₂(aq)</td><td>no reaction</td><td>no reaction</td><td>no reaction</td></tr></table></div>\n<div class=\"rxn\">Cl₂(aq) + 2Br⁻(aq) → 2Cl⁻(aq) + Br₂(aq)</div>\n<p>Chlorine is reduced (0 → −1); bromide is oxidised (−1 → 0).</p>\n\n<h2>Disproportionation</h2>\n<p><strong>Disproportionation</strong> is a redox reaction in which the <strong>same element is both oxidised and reduced</strong>.</p>\n<h3>Chlorine and water</h3>\n<div class=\"rxn\">Cl₂(aq) + H₂O(l) ⇌ HCl(aq) + HClO(aq)</div>\n<p>Chlorine goes from 0 to −1 in HCl (reduced) and from 0 to +1 in HClO, chloric(I) acid (oxidised). In sunlight a different reaction happens: 2Cl₂ + 2H₂O → 4HCl + O₂.</p>\n<p><strong>Water treatment:</strong> chlorine is added to drinking water to kill bacteria (HClO and ClO⁻ are the active agents). Benefits: it prevents water-borne diseases such as cholera, and a residual amount keeps working through the pipes. Risks: chlorine is toxic, and it can react with organic matter in water to form chlorinated hydrocarbons, some of which may cause cancer. The public-health benefit is judged to far outweigh the risk.</p>\n<h3>Chlorine and cold, dilute sodium hydroxide</h3>\n<div class=\"rxn\">Cl₂(aq) + 2NaOH(aq) → NaCl(aq) + NaClO(aq) + H₂O(l)</div>\n<p>Chlorine goes from 0 to −1 (NaCl) and 0 to +1 (NaClO). The solution of sodium chlorate(I), NaClO, is <strong>bleach</strong>.</p>\n\n<h2>Testing for halide ions</h2>\n<p>Add <strong>dilute nitric acid</strong> first (to remove carbonate ions, which would also give a precipitate), then <strong>silver nitrate solution</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"rxn\">Ag⁺(aq) + X⁻(aq) → AgX(s)</div>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><th>Halide</th><th>Precipitate</th><th>Dilute NH₃(aq)</th><th>Concentrated NH₃(aq)</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Cl⁻</td><td>white AgCl</td><td>dissolves</td><td>dissolves</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Br⁻</td><td>cream AgBr</td><td>does not dissolve</td><td>dissolves</td></tr>\n<tr><td>I⁻</td><td>yellow AgI</td><td>does not dissolve</td><td>does not dissolve</td></tr></table></div>\n<p>The ammonia step confirms the identity, because the white, cream and yellow colours can be hard to tell apart. Don't acidify with HCl: it adds chloride ions.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"tip\"><p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> for disproportionation, give the oxidation numbers of the element in every species: \"chlorine is oxidised from 0 to +1 in HClO and reduced from 0 to −1 in HCl\".</p></blockquote>\n\n<h2>Explaining reactivity in detail</h2>\n<p>A halogen atom reacts by <strong>gaining</strong> an electron into its outer p sub-shell. Down the group:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>atomic radius increases, so an incoming electron is further from the nucleus;</li>\n<li>shielding increases;</li>\n<li>so the nuclear attraction for the incoming electron decreases, and the halogen gains an electron less easily.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>So oxidising power decreases: Cl₂ > Br₂ > I₂. This is the opposite of Group 2, where reactivity increases down the group because those atoms <em>lose</em> electrons.</p>\n<h2>Fluorine and astatine</h2>\n<p>Fluorine is a pale yellow gas, the most reactive halogen and the strongest oxidising agent. Astatine is radioactive and rare; you can predict that it is a dark solid, less reactive than iodine, and that AgAt would be an insoluble precipitate.</p>\n<h2>Halogens as oxidising agents: more examples</h2>\n<div class=\"wex\"><p><strong>Worked example.</strong> Chlorine is bubbled through aqueous potassium iodide. Give the observation and the ionic equation, and identify the oxidising agent.</p>\n<p>The colourless solution turns brown (I₂); adding cyclohexane gives a violet top layer.</p>\n<p>Cl₂(aq) + 2I⁻(aq) → 2Cl⁻(aq) + I₂(aq). Chlorine is the oxidising agent (0 → −1).</p></div>\n<h2>Bleach and disproportionation: calculations</h2>\n<div class=\"wex\"><p><strong>Worked example.</strong> In Cl₂ + 2NaOH → NaCl + NaClO + H₂O, show that chlorine disproportionates.</p>\n<p>Cl in Cl₂: 0. In NaCl: −1 (reduced). In NaClO: Na +1, O −2, so Cl = +1 (oxidised). The same element is both oxidised and reduced.</p></div>\n<p>With <strong>hot</strong> concentrated NaOH, chlorine disproportionates differently, forming chlorate(V): 3Cl₂ + 6NaOH → 5NaCl + NaClO₃ + 3H₂O (Cl goes to −1 and +5).</p>\n<h2>Check yourself</h2><p class=\"muted\">Try each one before opening the answer.</p><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Why does bromine not react with potassium chloride solution?</summary><div><p>Bromine is a weaker oxidising agent than chlorine: it can’t remove an electron from Cl⁻, so it can’t displace chlorine.</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Why is nitric acid added before silver nitrate in the halide test?</summary><div><p>To react with and remove carbonate (and hydroxide) ions, which would also form precipitates with Ag⁺ and give a false result.</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Explain the increase in boiling point from Cl₂ to I₂.</summary><div><p>Down the group the molecules have more electrons, so stronger London forces between molecules; more energy is needed to overcome them.</p></div></details>\n<h2>Qualitative analysis: identifying ions</h2>\n<p>Qualitative analysis means finding out <em>what</em> is present, rather than how much. At AS you need tests for four anions and one cation; transition metal cations are added in 5.3.2.</p>\n<h3>Carbonate ions, CO₃²⁻</h3>\n<p>Add a dilute acid (dilute nitric acid). <strong>Effervescence</strong> (fizzing); bubble the gas through <strong>limewater</strong>, which turns <strong>milky</strong> (cloudy white) because CO₂ forms a precipitate of CaCO₃.</p>\n<div class=\"rxn\">CO₃²⁻(aq) + 2H⁺(aq) → CO₂(g) + H₂O(l)<br>Ca(OH)₂(aq) + CO₂(g) → CaCO₃(s) + H₂O(l)</div>\n<h3>Sulfate ions, SO₄²⁻</h3>\n<p>Add <strong>barium chloride</strong> or <strong>barium nitrate</strong> solution: a <strong>white precipitate</strong> of barium sulfate.</p>\n<div class=\"rxn\">Ba²⁺(aq) + SO₄²⁻(aq) → BaSO₄(s)</div>\n<h3>Halide ions, Cl⁻, Br⁻, I⁻</h3>\n<p>Add dilute nitric acid, then <strong>silver nitrate</strong>: white AgCl, cream AgBr, yellow AgI, confirmed with ammonia solution (see 3.1.3).</p>\n<h3>Ammonium ions, NH₄⁺</h3>\n<p>Add <strong>sodium hydroxide</strong> solution and <strong>warm</strong> gently. Ammonia gas is released, which turns <strong>damp red litmus paper blue</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"rxn\">NH₄⁺(aq) + OH⁻(aq) → NH₃(g) + H₂O(l)</div>\n\n<h2>The correct order</h2>\n<p>When a solution might contain several anions, the tests must be done in this order: <strong>carbonate → sulfate → halide</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Carbonate ions form a white precipitate with barium ions (BaCO₃) and with silver ions (Ag₂CO₃), so they would give a false positive in the sulfate and halide tests. Test for carbonate first by adding dilute HNO₃ until there's no more fizzing; this also removes the carbonate.</li>\n<li>Sulfate ions form a slightly soluble precipitate with silver ions (Ag₂SO₄), so test for sulfate before halides. If sulfate is present, add excess Ba(NO₃)₂, filter off the BaSO₄, then test the filtrate for halides.</li>\n<li>Use <strong>nitric acid</strong> and <strong>barium nitrate</strong>, not HCl or BaCl₂, if you will later test for halides, so you don't add chloride ions yourself.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"wex\"><p><strong>Worked example.</strong> A white solid dissolves in water. With dilute HNO₃ there is no effervescence; with Ba(NO₃)₂(aq) a white precipitate forms; with NaOH(aq) on warming, a gas turns damp red litmus blue. Identify the solid.</p>\n<p>No carbonate. Sulfate present. Ammonium present. The solid is <strong>ammonium sulfate, (NH₄)₂SO₄</strong>.</p></div>\n<blockquote class=\"tip\"><p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> give observations, not conclusions, when asked what you would see: \"a white precipitate\", \"effervescence; the gas turns limewater milky\", not \"sulfate is present\".</p></blockquote>\n\n<h2>Why the tests work</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Carbonate</strong>: carbonate ions are a base and react with H⁺ to release CO₂. CO₂ is acidic, and reacts with calcium hydroxide (limewater) to form insoluble calcium carbonate, the milky precipitate.</li>\n<li><strong>Sulfate</strong>: barium sulfate is extremely insoluble, so it precipitates even from dilute solutions.</li>\n<li><strong>Halides</strong>: silver halides are insoluble; their solubility in ammonia decreases from AgCl to AgI.</li>\n<li><strong>Ammonium</strong>: OH⁻ removes H⁺ from NH₄⁺, releasing ammonia gas, which is alkaline.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Designing a sequence of tests</h2>\n<div class=\"wex\"><p><strong>Worked example.</strong> You have four unlabelled solutions: sodium carbonate, sodium sulfate, sodium chloride and ammonium chloride. Describe how to identify each one.</p>\n<p>1. Add dilute HNO₃ to a sample of each: the one that fizzes (CO₂ turns limewater milky) is <strong>sodium carbonate</strong>.</p>\n<p>2. To fresh samples of the other three, add Ba(NO₃)₂(aq): a white precipitate shows <strong>sodium sulfate</strong>.</p>\n<p>3. The remaining two both give a white precipitate with AgNO₃ (both chlorides). Warm each with NaOH(aq): the one that gives a gas turning damp red litmus blue is <strong>ammonium chloride</strong>; the other is <strong>sodium chloride</strong>.</p></div>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Using HCl to acidify, then testing for chloride.</li>\n<li>Using BaCl₂ for the sulfate test, then testing the same solution for halides.</li>\n<li>Saying \"limewater goes cloudy\" without first saying there is effervescence.</li>\n<li>Forgetting that the litmus paper must be <strong>damp</strong>: ammonia must dissolve to form OH⁻ before it changes colour.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Check yourself</h2><p class=\"muted\">Try each one before opening the answer.</p><details class=\"fold\"><summary>A solution gives a white precipitate with acidified silver nitrate that dissolves in dilute ammonia. What ion is present?</summary><div><p>Chloride, Cl⁻.</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Why must you test for carbonate before sulfate?</summary><div><p>Carbonate ions also form a white precipitate with barium ions (BaCO₃), so they must be removed (and identified) first.</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Give the ionic equation for the ammonium test.</summary><div><p>NH₄⁺(aq) + OH⁻(aq) → NH₃(g) + H₂O(l)</p></div></details>\n<h2>More to try</h2><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Describe what you would see when dilute HNO₃ is added to sodium carbonate solution and the gas is bubbled through limewater.</summary><div><p>Effervescence (fizzing); the limewater turns milky (cloudy white).</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>A solution gives a yellow precipitate with acidified AgNO₃ that does not dissolve in concentrated ammonia. Identify the anion.</summary><div><p>Iodide, I⁻.</p></div></details>\n<h2>Halide ions as reducing agents</h2>\n<p>Reducing power increases down the group because larger halide ions lose their outer electron more easily (further from the nucleus, more shielded). AQA tests this with the reactions of <strong>solid sodium halides with concentrated sulfuric acid</strong>:</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Halide</th><th>Products</th><th>Observations</th><th>What H₂SO₄ is reduced to</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>NaF, NaCl</td><td>HF / HCl + NaHSO₄</td><td>Misty white fumes</td><td>Not reduced (acid–base only)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>NaBr</td><td>HBr, then Br₂ and SO₂</td><td>Misty fumes; orange-brown vapour</td><td>SO₂ (S: +6 → +4)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>NaI</td><td>HI, then I₂, SO₂, S and H₂S</td><td>Misty fumes; purple vapour / black solid; yellow solid; smell of bad eggs</td><td>SO₂ (+4), S (0) and H₂S (−2)</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Example equations: NaCl + H₂SO₄ → NaHSO₄ + HCl; 2HBr + H₂SO₄ → Br₂ + SO₂ + 2H₂O; 8HI + H₂SO₄ → 4I₂ + H₂S + 4H₂O.</p>\n<h2>Chlorine and water treatment</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Cl₂ + H₂O ⇌ HCl + HClO (a disproportionation: Cl goes from 0 to −1 and +1). HClO kills bacteria.</li>\n<li>In sunlight: 2Cl₂ + 2H₂O → 4HCl + O₂.</li>\n<li>With cold, dilute NaOH: Cl₂ + 2NaOH → NaCl + NaClO + H₂O, making bleach.</li>\n<li>The benefits to health of chlorinated water outweigh the toxic effects of chlorine.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Check yourself (AQA extras)</h2><p class=\"muted\">Try each one before opening the answer.</p><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Why does NaCl not give SO₂ with concentrated sulfuric acid, but NaBr does?</summary><div><p>Cl⁻ is too weak a reducing agent to reduce sulfuric acid; Br⁻ is larger, loses its electron more easily and reduces S from +6 to +4.</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Give the observations when concentrated H₂SO₄ is added to solid NaI.</summary><div><p>Misty fumes (HI), purple vapour or a black solid (I₂), a yellow solid (S) and a smell of bad eggs (H₂S).</p></div></details>\n","terms":[["Disproportionation","A redox reaction in which the same element is both oxidised and reduced."],["Halogens","Group 7 elements; they gain one electron to form 1− halide ions."]],"qa":[["Appearance of Cl₂, Br₂ and I₂ at room temperature","Chlorine: pale green gas. Bromine: red-brown liquid. Iodine: shiny grey-black solid."],["Colours of Cl₂, Br₂ and I₂ in water","Pale green, orange, brown."],["Colours of Cl₂, Br₂ and I₂ in cyclohexane","Pale green, orange, violet."],["Trend in boiling point down group 7","Increases: more electrons, stronger London forces."],["Trend in reactivity (oxidising power) down group 7","Decreases: larger radius and more shielding make it harder to gain an electron."],["Cl₂ + 2Br⁻ →","2Cl⁻ + Br₂: the solution turns orange."],["Chlorine + water","Cl₂ + H₂O ⇌ HClO + HCl (disproportionation)."],["Chlorine + cold dilute NaOH","Cl₂ + 2NaOH → NaClO + NaCl + H₂O: makes bleach."],["Benefits and risks of chlorinating water","Kills bacteria in drinking water; chlorine is toxic and can form chlorinated hydrocarbons."],["Test for halide ions","Add dilute HNO₃ then AgNO₃(aq): AgCl white, AgBr cream, AgI yellow precipitate."],["Confirming the halide with ammonia","AgCl dissolves in dilute NH₃; AgBr only in concentrated NH₃; AgI is insoluble."],["Test for carbonate ions","Add dilute HNO₃: effervescence; the gas turns limewater milky (CO₂)."],["Test for sulfate ions","Add Ba²⁺(aq), e.g. Ba(NO₃)₂: white precipitate of BaSO₄."],["Test for ammonium ions","Warm with NaOH(aq): NH₃ gas turns damp red litmus blue."],["Order of anion tests and why","Carbonate, then sulfate, then halide: CO₃²⁻ gives a precipitate with Ba²⁺ and Ag⁺; SO₄²⁻ gives one with Ag⁺."],["Why use Ba(NO₃)₂ not BaCl₂ before testing for halides?","BaCl₂ would add Cl⁻ ions and give a false positive."],["Why acidify with HNO₃ not HCl in the halide test?","HCl would add Cl⁻ ions."],["Why add dilute acid before AgNO₃?","To remove carbonate ions, which would also give a precipitate."],["Silver halide precipitate colours","AgCl white, AgBr cream, AgI yellow."],["Ionic equation for the sulfate test","Ba²⁺(aq) + SO₄²⁻(aq) → BaSO₄(s)."],["Ionic equation for the ammonium test","NH₄⁺(aq) + OH⁻(aq) → NH₃(g) + H₂O(l)."],["Ionic equation for the carbonate test","CO₃²⁻ + 2H⁺ → CO₂ + H₂O."],["What do you see when concentrated H₂SO₄ is added to solid NaBr?","Misty fumes of HBr and an orange-brown vapour of Br₂ (SO₂ also forms)."],["Which products show that I⁻ reduces H₂SO₄ to −2?","H₂S, which smells of bad eggs."],["Write the equation for chlorine with water.","Cl₂ + H₂O ⇌ HCl + HClO"]],"eqs":[],"mcq":[{"q":"Which sodium halide reduces concentrated sulfuric acid to hydrogen sulfide?","o":["NaF","NaCl","NaBr","NaI"],"a":3,"e":"I⁻ is the strongest reducing halide: it reduces S from +6 all the way to −2 (H₂S)."}],"exam":[],"labs":[],"pags":["rp4"],"packs":[{"set":"Topic questions","label":"Inorganic Chemistry 1 questions","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Topic-Qs/AQA/Inorganic-I/OUP-Chapterwide/Inorganic%20Chemistry%201%20QP.pdf"},{"set":"Topic questions","label":"Inorganic Chemistry 1 mark scheme","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Topic-Qs/AQA/Inorganic-I/OUP-Chapterwide/Inorganic%20Chemistry%201%20MS.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"PMT notes: Group 7 the Halogens","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Inorganic-I/Detailed/2.3.%20Group%207%20the%20Halogens.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Knockhardy notes: Group 7 - Halogens, Halide Ions and Uses","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Inorganic-I/Knockhardy/2.3.%20Group%207%20-%20Halogens,%20Halide%20Ions%20and%20Uses.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Mind map: Group 7(17) the Halogens","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Inorganic-I/Mind-Maps/2.3.%20Group%207(17)%20the%20Halogens.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Definitions: Inorganic Chemistry 1","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Inorganic-I/Definitions%20-%20Inorganic%20Chemistry%20I%20-%20AQA%20Chemistry%20A-level.pdf"},{"set":"Required practical guides","label":"RP 4: Identification of cations and anions","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Practical-Skills/RP%2004%20-%20Identification%20of%20cations%20and%20anions.pdf"}]});
S.addTopic({"id":"3.2.4","mod":2,"as":false,"title":"Properties of Period 3 elements and their oxides","short":"Reactions of Na–S with water and oxygen; the oxides with water, acids and bases.","spec":["Reactions of Na and Mg with water","Reactions of Na, Mg, Al, Si, P and S with oxygen to form Na₂O, MgO, Al₂O₃, SiO₂, P₄O₁₀ and SO₂","Trends in the melting points of the oxides, explained by their structure and bonding","Reactions of the oxides with water and the pH of the solutions formed","Amphoteric Al₂O₃; equations for the reactions of the oxides with simple acids and bases"],"notes":"\n<h2>Reactions of the elements with water</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Sodium</strong> reacts vigorously with cold water: it fizzes, melts into a ball and moves on the surface. 2Na + 2H₂O → 2NaOH + H₂. The solution is strongly alkaline, pH 12–14.</li>\n<li><strong>Magnesium</strong> reacts very slowly with cold water: Mg + 2H₂O → Mg(OH)₂ + H₂. Mg(OH)₂ is only slightly soluble, so the pH is about 9–10. It reacts much faster with steam to give MgO + H₂.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Reactions of the elements with oxygen</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Equation</th><th>Observation</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Na</td><td>4Na + O₂ → 2Na₂O</td><td>Yellow flame, white solid</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Mg</td><td>2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO</td><td>Bright white flame, white solid</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Al</td><td>4Al + 3O₂ → 2Al₂O₃</td><td>White solid (the powder burns brightly)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Si</td><td>Si + O₂ → SiO₂</td><td>Slow, white solid</td></tr>\n<tr><td>P</td><td>4P + 5O₂ → P₄O₁₀</td><td>White flame, white smoke</td></tr>\n<tr><td>S</td><td>S + O₂ → SO₂</td><td>Blue flame, choking gas</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2>Melting points of the oxides</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Na₂O, MgO, Al₂O₃</strong>: giant ionic lattices with strong electrostatic attraction between oppositely charged ions, so high melting points. MgO is higher than Na₂O because Mg²⁺ has a greater charge (and is smaller) than Na⁺. Al₂O₃ is a little lower than expected because the small, highly charged Al³⁺ gives it some covalent character.</li>\n<li><strong>SiO₂</strong>: macromolecular (giant covalent); many strong covalent bonds must break, so a very high melting point.</li>\n<li><strong>P₄O₁₀, SO₂</strong>: simple molecular; only weak van der Waals forces and dipole–dipole forces between molecules, so low melting points. P₄O₁₀ is higher than SO₂ because its molecules are larger.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>The oxides with water</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Oxide</th><th>Equation</th><th>pH</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Na₂O</td><td>Na₂O + H₂O → 2NaOH</td><td>12–14</td></tr>\n<tr><td>MgO</td><td>MgO + H₂O → Mg(OH)₂</td><td>9–10 (slightly soluble)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Al₂O₃</td><td>insoluble</td><td>7</td></tr>\n<tr><td>SiO₂</td><td>insoluble</td><td>7</td></tr>\n<tr><td>P₄O₁₀</td><td>P₄O₁₀ + 6H₂O → 4H₃PO₄</td><td>0–1</td></tr>\n<tr><td>SO₂</td><td>SO₂ + H₂O → H₂SO₃ (and SO₃ + H₂O → H₂SO₄)</td><td>2–3 (SO₂); 0–1 (SO₃)</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p><strong>Ionic oxides are basic</strong>: they release oxide ions, which take protons from water (O²⁻ + H₂O → 2OH⁻). <strong>Simple molecular oxides are acidic</strong>: they react with water to form acids that release H⁺. <strong>Al₂O₃ is amphoteric</strong>: it reacts with both acids and bases.</p>\n<h2>The oxides with acids and bases</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Basic oxides with acids: Na₂O + 2HCl → 2NaCl + H₂O; MgO + H₂SO₄ → MgSO₄ + H₂O.</li>\n<li>Amphoteric Al₂O₃: with acid, Al₂O₃ + 6HCl → 2AlCl₃ + 3H₂O; with hot concentrated base, Al₂O₃ + 2NaOH + 3H₂O → 2NaAl(OH)₄.</li>\n<li>SiO₂ is acidic but insoluble; it reacts with hot concentrated NaOH: SiO₂ + 2NaOH → Na₂SiO₃ + H₂O.</li>\n<li>P₄O₁₀ + 12NaOH → 4Na₃PO₄ + 6H₂O; SO₂ + 2NaOH → Na₂SO₃ + H₂O; SO₃ + 2NaOH → Na₂SO₄ + H₂O.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote class=\"tip\"><p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> when you explain a melting point, always name the structure (giant ionic / macromolecular / simple molecular), the force or bond that must be overcome, and its relative strength.</p></blockquote>\n<h2>Check yourself (AQA extras)</h2><p class=\"muted\">Try each one before opening the answer.</p><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Why does MgO have a higher melting point than Na₂O?</summary><div><p>Both are giant ionic lattices, but Mg²⁺ has a higher charge and smaller radius than Na⁺, so the electrostatic attraction between the ions is stronger and more energy is needed to overcome it.</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Write an equation for P₄O₁₀ reacting with water and state the pH.</summary><div><p>P₄O₁₀ + 6H₂O → 4H₃PO₄; about pH 0–1.</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Show that Al₂O₃ is amphoteric.</summary><div><p>Al₂O₃ + 6HCl → 2AlCl₃ + 3H₂O (acting as a base) and Al₂O₃ + 2NaOH + 3H₂O → 2NaAl(OH)₄ (acting as an acid).</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Why is SiO₂ classed as acidic even though it does not dissolve in water?</summary><div><p>It reacts with bases such as hot concentrated NaOH to form a salt (sodium silicate).</p></div></details>\n","terms":[["Amphoteric","Able to react as both an acid and a base, as aluminium oxide does."],["Basic oxide","An ionic oxide whose O²⁻ ions accept protons, e.g. Na₂O and MgO."],["Acidic oxide","A covalent oxide that reacts with water to form an acid or reacts with bases, e.g. P₄O₁₀, SO₂, SiO₂."]],"qa":[["Write the equation for sodium with water and give the pH.","2Na + 2H₂O → 2NaOH + H₂; pH 12–14."],["Why does SiO₂ have a very high melting point?","It is macromolecular: many strong covalent bonds must be broken."],["Why do P₄O₁₀ and SO₂ have low melting points?","They are simple molecular; only weak intermolecular forces need overcoming."],["Write the equation for SO₂ with water.","SO₂ + H₂O → H₂SO₃"],["Write the equation for Al₂O₃ with NaOH.","Al₂O₃ + 2NaOH + 3H₂O → 2NaAl(OH)₄"],["What is seen when sulfur burns in oxygen?","A blue flame and a choking gas (SO₂)."]],"eqs":[],"mcq":[{"q":"Which Period 3 oxide is amphoteric?","o":["MgO","Al₂O₃","SiO₂","P₄O₁₀"],"a":1,"e":"Aluminium oxide reacts with both acids and bases."},{"q":"What is the approximate pH of the solution formed when P₄O₁₀ is added to water?","o":["0–1","7","9–10","13–14"],"a":0,"e":"P₄O₁₀ + 6H₂O → 4H₃PO₄, a fairly strong acid in solution."},{"q":"Which oxide has a giant covalent (macromolecular) structure?","o":["Na₂O","Al₂O₃","SiO₂","SO₂"],"a":2,"e":"SiO₂ is macromolecular, like diamond."}],"exam":[],"labs":[],"pags":[],"packs":[{"set":"Topic questions","label":"Inorganic Chemistry 2 questions","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Topic-Qs/AQA/Inorganic-II/OUP-Chapterwide/Inorganic%20Chemistry%202%20QP.pdf"},{"set":"Topic questions","label":"Inorganic Chemistry 2 mark scheme","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Topic-Qs/AQA/Inorganic-II/OUP-Chapterwide/Inorganic%20Chemistry%202%20MS.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"PMT notes: Period 3 Elements","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Inorganic-II/Detailed/2.4.%20Period%203%20Elements.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Mind map: Properties of Period 3 Elements and Their Oxides","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Inorganic-II/Mind-Maps/2.4.%20Properties%20of%20Period%203%20Elements%20and%20Their%20Oxides.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Definitions: Inorganic Chemistry 2","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Inorganic-II/Definitions%20-%20Inorganic%20Chemistry%20II%20-%20AQA%20Chemistry%20A-level.pdf"}]});
S.addTopic({"id":"3.2.5","mod":2,"as":false,"title":"Transition metals","short":"Complexes, shapes, isomerism, colour, variable oxidation states, redox titrations and catalysts.","spec":["Transition metal characteristics (Ti–Cu) arise from an incomplete d sub-level: complexes, coloured ions, variable oxidation states and catalytic activity","Ligands: monodentate, bidentate (e.g. ethanedioate, NH₂CH₂CH₂NH₂) and multidentate (EDTA⁴⁻); co-ordination number","Substitution reactions and the chelate effect, explained with entropy; haem and haemoglobin","Shapes of complexes (octahedral, tetrahedral, square planar, linear) and cis–trans and optical isomerism; cisplatin","Formation of coloured ions: ΔE = hν; colorimetry to find concentration","Variable oxidation states: vanadium, Tollens’ reagent, redox titrations with KMnO₄ and K₂Cr₂O₇","Heterogeneous and homogeneous catalysts; autocatalysis by Mn²⁺; catalyst poisoning"],"notes":"\n<h2>What a transition element is</h2>\n<p>A <strong>transition element</strong> is a <strong>d-block element that forms at least one ion with an incomplete d sub-shell</strong>. Ti to Cu in period 4 are transition elements. Scandium (Sc³⁺ is 3d⁰) and zinc (Zn²⁺ is 3d¹⁰) are d-block elements but <strong>not</strong> transition elements, because their only ions have an empty or full d sub-shell.</p>\n<h3>Electron configurations</h3>\n<p>The d-block fills 3d after 4s, with two exceptions: <strong>Cr [Ar] 3d⁵ 4s¹</strong> and <strong>Cu [Ar] 3d¹⁰ 4s¹</strong>. When ions form, the <strong>4s electrons are lost first</strong>: Cu²⁺ is [Ar] 3d⁹; Mn²⁺ is [Ar] 3d⁵; Fe³⁺ is [Ar] 3d⁵.</p>\n\n<h2>Characteristic properties</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Variable oxidation states</strong>: the 4s and 3d sub-shells are close in energy, so different numbers of electrons can be lost. Manganese shows +2 to +7.</li>\n<li><strong>Coloured compounds and ions</strong> in solution: Cu²⁺(aq) blue, Fe²⁺(aq) pale green, Fe³⁺(aq) yellow/orange-brown, Cr³⁺(aq) green or violet, MnO₄⁻ purple, Cr₂O₇²⁻ orange.</li>\n<li><strong>Catalysts</strong>, because they can change oxidation state (accepting and donating electrons) and provide surfaces where reactions happen. Fe in the Haber process; V₂O₅ in the Contact process (making SO₃); MnO₂ decomposing H₂O₂; Ni for hydrogenation; Cu²⁺ catalyses the zinc–acid reaction.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Complex ions</h2>\n<p>A <strong>complex ion</strong> is a central metal ion surrounded by <strong>ligands</strong> bonded by <strong>coordinate (dative covalent) bonds</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>ligand</strong> is a molecule or ion that <strong>donates a pair of electrons</strong> to a central metal ion to form a coordinate bond.</li>\n<li><strong>Monodentate</strong> ligands form one coordinate bond: H₂O, NH₃, Cl⁻, CN⁻, OH⁻.</li>\n<li><strong>Bidentate</strong> ligands form two: ethane-1,2-diamine (NH₂CH₂CH₂NH₂, \"en\") and ethanedioate (C₂O₄²⁻, oxalate).</li>\n<li>The <strong>coordination number</strong> is the number of coordinate bonds to the central metal ion (not the number of ligands: three bidentate ligands give coordination number 6).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Shapes</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><th>Coordination number</th><th>Shape</th><th>Angle</th><th>Example</th></tr>\n<tr><td>6</td><td>octahedral</td><td>90°</td><td>[Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺, [Co(NH₃)₆]²⁺</td></tr>\n<tr><td>4</td><td>tetrahedral</td><td>109.5°</td><td>[CuCl₄]²⁻, [CoCl₄]²⁻ (with large Cl⁻ ligands)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>4</td><td>square planar</td><td>90°</td><td>[Pt(NH₃)₂Cl₂] (cisplatin)</td></tr></table></div>\n<p>The overall charge of a complex = charge on the metal ion + total charge of the ligands: [CuCl₄]²⁻ is Cu²⁺ + 4Cl⁻.</p>\n\n<h2>Stereoisomerism in complexes</h2>\n<h3>Cis–trans isomerism</h3>\n<p>Square planar complexes with two pairs of different ligands (MA₂B₂), and octahedral complexes of type MA₄B₂, can have cis (same side, 90° apart) and trans (opposite, 180°) isomers.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cisplatin</strong>, cis-[Pt(NH₃)₂Cl₂], is an <strong>anticancer drug</strong>. It binds to DNA in cancer cells (the two Cl⁻ ligands are replaced by nitrogen atoms on adjacent guanine bases), preventing the DNA from replicating so the cell dies. Transplatin can't bind this way and is not effective. Cisplatin also harms healthy cells, causing side effects such as hair loss and kidney damage, so doses are kept low.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Optical isomerism</h3>\n<p>Octahedral complexes with <strong>three bidentate ligands</strong> (e.g. [Ni(en)₃]²⁺), or two bidentate ligands with two others in a cis arrangement, exist as <strong>non-superimposable mirror images</strong>: optical isomers.</p>\n\n<h2>Ligand substitution</h2>\n<p>One ligand can be replaced by another. Learn these colour changes:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><th>Reaction</th><th>Colour change</th></tr>\n<tr><td>[Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺ + 4NH₃ → [Cu(NH₃)₄(H₂O)₂]²⁺ + 4H₂O<br>(excess aqueous ammonia)</td><td>pale blue → <strong>dark (deep) blue</strong> solution</td></tr>\n<tr><td>[Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺ + 4Cl⁻ ⇌ [CuCl₄]²⁻ + 6H₂O<br>(concentrated HCl)</td><td>pale blue → <strong>yellow</strong> (often looks green as a mixture); octahedral → tetrahedral</td></tr>\n<tr><td>[Cr(H₂O)₆]³⁺ + 6NH₃ → [Cr(NH₃)₆]³⁺ + 6H₂O<br>(excess aqueous ammonia)</td><td>violet (green in practice) → <strong>purple</strong> solution</td></tr></table></div>\n<h3>Haemoglobin</h3>\n<p>Haemoglobin contains Fe²⁺ complexes. <strong>Oxygen</strong> binds to the iron as a ligand in the lungs and is released in tissues. <strong>Carbon monoxide</strong> forms a much <strong>stronger</strong> coordinate bond with Fe²⁺ than O₂ does, replacing it by ligand substitution. The CO isn't released, so haemoglobin can't carry oxygen: this is why CO is toxic.</p>\n\n<h2>Precipitation reactions</h2>\n<p>Adding aqueous sodium hydroxide or ammonia to a transition metal ion solution forms a <strong>hydroxide precipitate</strong>. Learn the colours and what happens in excess:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><th>Ion</th><th>NaOH(aq), a few drops</th><th>Excess NaOH(aq)</th><th>NH₃(aq), a few drops</th><th>Excess NH₃(aq)</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Cu²⁺</td><td>blue ppt Cu(OH)₂</td><td>no change</td><td>blue ppt</td><td>dissolves → dark blue solution [Cu(NH₃)₄(H₂O)₂]²⁺</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Fe²⁺</td><td>green ppt Fe(OH)₂ (turns orange-brown at the surface in air)</td><td>no change</td><td>green ppt</td><td>no change</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Fe³⁺</td><td>orange-brown ppt Fe(OH)₃</td><td>no change</td><td>orange-brown ppt</td><td>no change</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Mn²⁺</td><td>light brown ppt Mn(OH)₂ (darkens in air)</td><td>no change</td><td>light brown ppt</td><td>no change</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cr³⁺</td><td>grey-green ppt Cr(OH)₃</td><td>dissolves → dark green solution [Cr(OH)₆]³⁻</td><td>grey-green ppt</td><td>dissolves → purple solution [Cr(NH₃)₆]³⁺</td></tr></table></div>\n<div class=\"rxn\">Cu²⁺(aq) + 2OH⁻(aq) → Cu(OH)₂(s)<br>Fe³⁺(aq) + 3OH⁻(aq) → Fe(OH)₃(s)</div>\n\n<h2>Redox reactions of transition metal ions</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Fe²⁺ ⇌ Fe³⁺</strong>: Fe²⁺ is oxidised to Fe³⁺ by acidified MnO₄⁻ (purple → colourless); Fe³⁺ is reduced to Fe²⁺ by I⁻ (2Fe³⁺ + 2I⁻ → 2Fe²⁺ + I₂, solution turns brown).</li>\n<li><strong>Cr₂O₇²⁻ → Cr³⁺</strong>: dichromate(VI) is reduced by zinc in acid (orange → green), and further to Cr²⁺ (blue) with excess zinc. Cr³⁺ is oxidised back to CrO₄²⁻ (yellow) by hot alkaline H₂O₂.</li>\n<li><strong>Cu²⁺ → Cu⁺</strong>: Cu²⁺ reacts with I⁻ to form a white precipitate of CuI and brown I₂: 2Cu²⁺ + 4I⁻ → 2CuI + I₂. Copper(I) ions disproportionate in solution: 2Cu⁺ → Cu²⁺ + Cu.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>You may be asked to interpret unfamiliar redox reactions of other transition metals using oxidation numbers and half-equations.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"tip\"><p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> \"ligand\" and \"coordinate bond\" must appear in a definition of a complex ion. When a question asks for the shape of a complex, give the coordination number and bond angle as well.</p></blockquote>\n\n<h2>Check yourself</h2><p class=\"muted\">Try each one before opening the answer.</p><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Why is zinc not a transition element?</summary><div><p>Its only ion, Zn²⁺, has a full 3d sub-shell (3d¹⁰), so no ion has an incomplete d sub-shell.</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>What is the coordination number of [Co(en)₃]³⁺, and its shape?</summary><div><p>6 (three bidentate ligands, each forming 2 bonds); octahedral.</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Describe what you see when excess aqueous ammonia is added to copper(II) sulfate solution.</summary><div><p>A pale blue precipitate forms, then dissolves to give a dark blue solution.</p></div></details>\n<h2>AQA transition metal details</h2>\n<h3>The chelate effect</h3>\n<p>Replacing monodentate ligands with a multidentate one increases the number of particles: [Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺ + EDTA⁴⁻ → [CuEDTA]²⁻ + 6H₂O makes 7 particles from 2. The <strong>entropy increases</strong>, and because the bonds broken and made are similar (ΔH ≈ 0), ΔG is negative and the chelate complex is more stable.</p>\n<h3>Haem</h3>\n<p>Haem is an iron(II) complex with a multidentate ligand. Oxygen binds to the Fe²⁺ to be carried in the blood. Carbon monoxide is toxic because it replaces the oxygen and binds more strongly.</p>\n<h3>Colour and colorimetry</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Ligands split the d orbitals into two energy levels. Electrons absorb visible light to move to the higher level: ΔE = hν = hc/λ. The colour seen is the light that is not absorbed.</li>\n<li>ΔE (and so the colour) changes with the metal’s oxidation state, the ligand and the co-ordination number.</li>\n<li><strong>Colorimetry</strong>: measure the absorbance of standard solutions to draw a calibration curve, then read off the concentration of the unknown. Use a filter of the complementary colour.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Vanadium oxidation states</h3>\n<p>Zinc in acid reduces vanadium(V) step by step: VO₂⁺ (yellow, +5) → VO²⁺ (blue, +4) → V³⁺ (green, +3) → V²⁺ (violet, +2).</p>\n<h3>Tollens’ reagent</h3>\n<p>[Ag(NH₃)₂]⁺ is reduced to silver by aldehydes: the “silver mirror” test.</p>\n<h3>Redox titrations</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>MnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ + 5Fe²⁺ → Mn²⁺ + 4H₂O + 5Fe³⁺ (self-indicating: the first permanent pink colour is the end point).</li>\n<li>Cr₂O₇²⁻ + 14H⁺ + 6Fe²⁺ → 2Cr³⁺ + 7H₂O + 6Fe³⁺.</li>\n<li>2MnO₄⁻ + 16H⁺ + 5C₂O₄²⁻ → 2Mn²⁺ + 8H₂O + 10CO₂.</li>\n<li>Acidify with dilute sulfuric acid, not HCl (which is oxidised by MnO₄⁻) or HNO₃ (an oxidising agent itself).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Catalysts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Heterogeneous</strong> catalysts are in a different phase from the reactants and work by adsorption onto active sites. Supports (e.g. a ceramic honeycomb) increase the surface area and save expensive metal. Examples: iron in the Haber process; V₂O₅ in the Contact process (V₂O₅ + SO₂ → V₂O₄ + SO₃, then V₂O₄ + ½O₂ → V₂O₅).</li>\n<li>Catalysts can be <strong>poisoned</strong> by impurities (e.g. sulfur) that block the active sites, which adds cost.</li>\n<li><strong>Homogeneous</strong> catalysts are in the same phase and work through an intermediate species. Fe²⁺ catalyses the reaction between I⁻ and S₂O₈²⁻: the variable oxidation state lets Fe²⁺ be oxidised to Fe³⁺ and then reduced back. This route avoids the slow collision of two negative ions.</li>\n<li><strong>Autocatalysis</strong>: Mn²⁺ catalyses the reaction between C₂O₄²⁻ and MnO₄⁻, so the reaction speeds up after it starts.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Check yourself (AQA extras)</h2><p class=\"muted\">Try each one before opening the answer.</p><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Explain the chelate effect in terms of entropy.</summary><div><p>A multidentate ligand replaces several monodentate ligands, increasing the number of particles in solution. The entropy increases, so ΔG is more negative and the complex formed is more stable.</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Why is sulfuric acid used, not hydrochloric acid, in permanganate titrations?</summary><div><p>MnO₄⁻ would oxidise Cl⁻ to Cl₂, using up extra permanganate and making the titre too large.</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>State the colours of vanadium in the +5, +4, +3 and +2 states.</summary><div><p>Yellow (VO₂⁺), blue (VO²⁺), green (V³⁺), violet (V²⁺).</p></div></details>\n","terms":[["Transition element","A d-block element that forms an ion with an incomplete d sub-shell."],["Ligand","A molecule or ion that donates a pair of electrons to a central metal ion to form a coordinate bond."],["Coordination number","The number of coordinate bonds to the central metal ion."],["Complex ion","A transition metal ion bonded to one or more ligands by coordinate bonds."],["Bidentate ligand","A ligand that forms two coordinate bonds, e.g. ethane-1,2-diamine (en) or ethanedioate."],["Ligand substitution","One ligand in a complex ion is replaced by another."],["Chelate effect","Multidentate ligands form more stable complexes than monodentate ones because substitution increases the number of particles and so the entropy."],["Heterogeneous catalyst","A catalyst in a different phase from the reactants, working by adsorption onto active sites."],["Homogeneous catalyst","A catalyst in the same phase as the reactants, working through an intermediate species."],["Autocatalysis","A reaction catalysed by one of its own products, e.g. Mn²⁺ in the oxidation of ethanedioate by manganate(VII)."]],"qa":[["Characteristic properties of transition elements","Variable oxidation states, coloured ions, catalytic activity, form complex ions."],["Why are Sc and Zn not transition elements?","Sc³⁺ has an empty d sub-shell (3d⁰); Zn²⁺ has a full one (3d¹⁰)."],["Shapes of 6- and 4-coordinate complexes","6: octahedral, 90°. 4: tetrahedral 109.5° (e.g. [CuCl₄]²⁻) or square planar 90° (e.g. cisplatin)."],["Isomerism in complex ions","Cis–trans in square planar and octahedral; optical isomerism in octahedral complexes with bidentate ligands."],["Action of cisplatin","Binds to DNA in cancer cells, preventing cell division."],["[Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺ + excess conc. HCl","[CuCl₄]²⁻: pale blue to yellow solution (tetrahedral)."],["[Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺ + excess NH₃(aq)","[Cu(NH₃)₄(H₂O)₂]²⁺: deep blue solution (after a pale blue precipitate)."],["Haemoglobin and carbon monoxide","CO binds more strongly than O₂ to the Fe²⁺ in haem, so oxygen cannot be carried."],["Cr₂O₇²⁻ + Zn / acid","Reduced to Cr³⁺ (green) then Cr²⁺ (blue)."],["Fe³⁺ + I⁻","2Fe³⁺ + 2I⁻ → 2Fe²⁺ + I₂: brown colour of iodine."],["Cu²⁺ + I⁻","2Cu²⁺ + 4I⁻ → 2CuI + I₂: white precipitate in a brown solution."],["Disproportionation of Cu⁺ in acid","2Cu⁺ → Cu²⁺ + Cu: blue solution and brown solid."],["What are the colours of vanadium in oxidation states +5, +4, +3 and +2?","Yellow, blue, green, violet."],["Which catalyst is used in the Contact process?","Vanadium(V) oxide, V₂O₅."],["What does Fe²⁺ catalyse in a homogeneous reaction on the AQA spec?","The reaction between I⁻ and S₂O₈²⁻ ions."],["Why is CO toxic?","It binds to the Fe²⁺ in haemoglobin more strongly than oxygen, so oxygen cannot be carried."]],"eqs":[],"mcq":[{"q":"Which catalyst is an example of autocatalysis?","o":["Fe in the Haber process","V₂O₅ in the Contact process","Mn²⁺ in the MnO₄⁻/C₂O₄²⁻ reaction","Ni in hydrogenation"],"a":2,"e":"Mn²⁺ is a product of the reaction it catalyses."}],"exam":[],"labs":[],"pags":[],"packs":[{"set":"Topic questions","label":"Inorganic Chemistry 2 questions","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Topic-Qs/AQA/Inorganic-II/OUP-Chapterwide/Inorganic%20Chemistry%202%20QP.pdf"},{"set":"Topic questions","label":"Inorganic Chemistry 2 mark scheme","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Topic-Qs/AQA/Inorganic-II/OUP-Chapterwide/Inorganic%20Chemistry%202%20MS.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"PMT notes: Transition Metals","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Inorganic-II/Detailed/2.5.%20Transition%20Metals.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Knockhardy notes: Transition Metals - Oxidation, Coloured Ions, Colorimetry, Ligands, Coordination Number, Isomerism in Complexes and Catalysis","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Inorganic-II/Knockhardy/2.5.%20Transition%20Metals%20-%20Oxidation,%20Coloured%20Ions,%20Colorimetry,%20Ligands,%20Coordination%20Number,%20Isomerism%20in%20Complexes%20and%20Catalysis.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Mind map: Transition Metals","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Inorganic-II/Mind-Maps/2.5.%20Transition%20Metals.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Definitions: Inorganic Chemistry 2","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Inorganic-II/Definitions%20-%20Inorganic%20Chemistry%20II%20-%20AQA%20Chemistry%20A-level.pdf"}]});
S.addTopic({"id":"3.2.6","mod":2,"as":false,"title":"Reactions of ions in aqueous solution","short":"Metal–aqua ions: acidity, hydroxide and carbonate precipitates, and ligand substitution.","spec":["The metal–aqua ions [M(H₂O)₆]²⁺ and [M(H₂O)₆]³⁺ in water","Why [M(H₂O)₆]³⁺ solutions are more acidic than [M(H₂O)₆]²⁺ (charge density and polarisation)","Reactions of Fe²⁺, Cu²⁺, Fe³⁺ and Al³⁺ aqua ions with NaOH, NH₃ and Na₂CO₃: colours of precipitates and solutions","Amphoteric Al(OH)₃","Required practical 11: simple test-tube reactions to identify transition metal ions in aqueous solution"],"notes":"\n<h2>Identifying ions: the full set</h2>\n<p>At A level you combine the anion tests from 3.1.4 with cation tests, including transition metal ions. You need to describe tests, give observations, and deduce the identity of unknown substances.</p>\n<h3>Anions</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><th>Ion</th><th>Test</th><th>Positive result</th></tr>\n<tr><td>CO₃²⁻</td><td>dilute HNO₃; gas into limewater</td><td>effervescence; limewater turns milky</td></tr>\n<tr><td>SO₄²⁻</td><td>Ba(NO₃)₂(aq) or BaCl₂(aq)</td><td>white precipitate</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cl⁻ / Br⁻ / I⁻</td><td>dilute HNO₃ then AgNO₃(aq); then NH₃(aq)</td><td>white / cream / yellow ppt; dissolve in dilute / concentrated / neither NH₃</td></tr></table></div>\n<p>Order: carbonate, then sulfate, then halide (see 3.1.4).</p>\n<h3>Cations</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><th>Ion</th><th>Test</th><th>Positive result</th></tr>\n<tr><td>NH₄⁺</td><td>warm with NaOH(aq)</td><td>gas turns damp red litmus blue</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cu²⁺</td><td>NaOH(aq); NH₃(aq) in excess</td><td>blue ppt; dissolves in excess NH₃ to a dark blue solution</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Fe²⁺</td><td>NaOH(aq)</td><td>green ppt, slowly turning orange-brown at the surface</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Fe³⁺</td><td>NaOH(aq)</td><td>orange-brown ppt</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Mn²⁺</td><td>NaOH(aq)</td><td>light brown ppt, darkening in air</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cr³⁺</td><td>NaOH(aq) in excess</td><td>grey-green ppt; dissolves in excess to a dark green solution</td></tr></table></div>\n<div class=\"wex\"><p><strong>Worked example.</strong> A green solid dissolves to give a pale green solution. With NaOH(aq) a green precipitate forms, turning brown at the surface on standing. With HNO₃ and Ba(NO₃)₂, a white precipitate. Identify the solid.</p>\n<p>Green ppt turning orange-brown: Fe²⁺. White ppt with barium ions: SO₄²⁻. The solid is <strong>iron(II) sulfate, FeSO₄</strong>.</p></div>\n<blockquote class=\"tip\"><p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> these questions reward precise observations: name the colour, say \"precipitate\" or \"solution\", and say what happens in excess. \"Turns blue\" is ambiguous; \"pale blue precipitate that dissolves in excess ammonia to form a dark blue solution\" scores fully.</p></blockquote>\n\n<h2>Why the precipitates form</h2>\n<p>In water, a transition metal ion exists as an aqua complex, e.g. [Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺. Hydroxide ions (from NaOH, or from the reaction NH₃ + H₂O ⇌ NH₄⁺ + OH⁻) remove H⁺ from the water ligands, giving a neutral complex that is insoluble:</p>\n<div class=\"rxn\">[Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺ + 2OH⁻ → [Cu(OH)₂(H₂O)₄] + 2H₂O &nbsp;&nbsp;(simplified: Cu²⁺ + 2OH⁻ → Cu(OH)₂)</div>\n<p>In <strong>excess ammonia</strong>, ammonia ligands replace water and hydroxide in copper(II) and chromium(III), forming soluble complex ions, so the precipitate dissolves. In <strong>excess NaOH</strong>, only Cr(OH)₃ dissolves (forming [Cr(OH)₆]³⁻), because it is amphoteric.</p>\n<h2>Why Fe(OH)₂ turns brown</h2>\n<p>Iron(II) is easily oxidised by oxygen in the air to iron(III): the green Fe(OH)₂ precipitate slowly becomes orange-brown Fe(OH)₃, starting at the surface where it meets the air. Mn(OH)₂ darkens for the same reason (oxidised to manganese(IV)).</p>\n<h2>A full identification problem</h2>\n<div class=\"wex\"><p><strong>Worked example.</strong> Solid X is green. A solution of X with a few drops of NaOH(aq) gives a grey-green precipitate, which dissolves in excess to a dark green solution. Acidified with HNO₃ then tested with AgNO₃, it gives a white precipitate that dissolves in dilute ammonia. Identify X and write ionic equations.</p>\n<p>Grey-green precipitate dissolving in excess NaOH: <strong>Cr³⁺</strong>. White precipitate soluble in dilute NH₃: <strong>Cl⁻</strong>. X is <strong>chromium(III) chloride, CrCl₃</strong>.</p>\n<p>Cr³⁺(aq) + 3OH⁻(aq) → Cr(OH)₃(s); Cr(OH)₃(s) + 3OH⁻(aq) → [Cr(OH)₆]³⁻(aq); Ag⁺(aq) + Cl⁻(aq) → AgCl(s).</p></div>\n<h2>Check yourself</h2><p class=\"muted\">Try each one before opening the answer.</p><details class=\"fold\"><summary>How could you distinguish Fe²⁺ from Fe³⁺ using NaOH(aq)?</summary><div><p>Fe²⁺ gives a green precipitate (turning brown on standing); Fe³⁺ gives an orange-brown precipitate straight away.</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>A blue precipitate dissolves in excess ammonia to a dark blue solution. Which ion?</summary><div><p>Cu²⁺ (forming [Cu(NH₃)₄(H₂O)₂]²⁺).</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Which test distinguishes NH₄Cl from NaCl?</summary><div><p>Warm with NaOH(aq): NH₄Cl gives ammonia, which turns damp red litmus blue; NaCl gives no gas.</p></div></details>\n<h2>Flame colours and other quick identifications</h2>\n<p>You may meet these in Paper 3 contexts: lithium red, sodium yellow-orange, potassium lilac, calcium brick red, copper blue-green (flame tests are a GCSE technique you can still use). Hydrated copper(II) sulfate is blue and anhydrous copper(II) sulfate is white: adding water turns it blue, a test for water.</p>\n<h2>More to try</h2><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Which hydroxide precipitate is light brown and darkens on standing?</summary><div><p>Mn(OH)₂, from Mn²⁺.</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Write the ionic equation for the precipitate formed when NaOH(aq) is added to iron(III) chloride solution.</summary><div><p>Fe³⁺(aq) + 3OH⁻(aq) → Fe(OH)₃(s)</p></div></details>\n<h2>Acidity of metal–aqua ions</h2>\n<p>In water, metal ions form <strong>hexaaqua ions</strong>, [M(H₂O)₆]²⁺ and [M(H₂O)₆]³⁺. These act as Brønsted–Lowry acids by giving a proton to water (hydrolysis):</p>\n$$[\\mathrm{Fe(H_2O)_6}]^{3+}+\\mathrm{H_2O}\\rightleftharpoons[\\mathrm{Fe(H_2O)_5(OH)}]^{2+}+\\mathrm{H_3O^+}$$\n<p>The <strong>3+ ions are more acidic</strong> than the 2+ ions: M³⁺ is smaller and more highly charged, so it has a higher charge density. It polarises (attracts electrons from) the O–H bonds in the water ligands more strongly, which weakens them and releases H⁺ more easily.</p>\n<h2>Reactions with hydroxide ions and ammonia</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Ion (colour)</th><th>A little NaOH or NH₃</th><th>Excess NaOH</th><th>Excess NH₃</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>[Fe(H₂O)₆]²⁺ (pale green)</td><td>Green precipitate Fe(H₂O)₄(OH)₂, darkens to brown in air</td><td>No change</td><td>No change</td></tr>\n<tr><td>[Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺ (blue)</td><td>Blue precipitate Cu(H₂O)₄(OH)₂</td><td>No change</td><td>Deep blue solution [Cu(NH₃)₄(H₂O)₂]²⁺</td></tr>\n<tr><td>[Fe(H₂O)₆]³⁺ (yellow / brown)</td><td>Brown precipitate Fe(H₂O)₃(OH)₃</td><td>No change</td><td>No change</td></tr>\n<tr><td>[Al(H₂O)₆]³⁺ (colourless)</td><td>White precipitate Al(H₂O)₃(OH)₃</td><td>Colourless solution [Al(OH)₄]⁻</td><td>No change</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Ammonia acts as a base here (it removes protons), except with copper, where excess ammonia also acts as a ligand and substitutes four water molecules.</p>\n<p><strong>Amphoteric Al(OH)₃</strong>: it dissolves in acid, Al(H₂O)₃(OH)₃ + 3H⁺ → [Al(H₂O)₆]³⁺, and in excess hydroxide, Al(H₂O)₃(OH)₃ + OH⁻ → [Al(OH)₄]⁻ + 3H₂O.</p>\n<h2>Reactions with carbonate ions</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>M²⁺ ions</strong> are not acidic enough to react with CO₃²⁻ to release CO₂, so they form a metal carbonate precipitate: [Fe(H₂O)₆]²⁺ + CO₃²⁻ → FeCO₃ + 6H₂O (green); CuCO₃ is green-blue.</li>\n<li><strong>M³⁺ ions</strong> are acidic enough to release CO₂: you see <strong>effervescence</strong> and a hydroxide precipitate. 2[Fe(H₂O)₆]³⁺ + 3CO₃²⁻ → 2Fe(H₂O)₃(OH)₃ + 3CO₂ + 3H₂O (brown precipitate); aluminium gives a white precipitate.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Ligand substitution with chloride ions</h2>\n<p>Adding concentrated HCl to [Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺ gives the yellow-green tetrahedral [CuCl₄]²⁻. Chloride ions are larger than water molecules, so only four fit around the metal and the co-ordination number changes from 6 to 4.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"tip\"><p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> always give both the formula of the complex and its colour, and say “precipitate” or “solution”. For excess reagent, say what dissolves and what colour the solution becomes.</p></blockquote>\n<h2>Check yourself (AQA extras)</h2><p class=\"muted\">Try each one before opening the answer.</p><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Why is a solution of [Al(H₂O)₆]³⁺ more acidic than one of [Fe(H₂O)₆]²⁺?</summary><div><p>Al³⁺ has a higher charge and a smaller radius, so a higher charge density. It polarises the O–H bonds in its water ligands more, so H⁺ is released more readily.</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>What do you see when Na₂CO₃ (aq) is added to iron(III) chloride solution?</summary><div><p>Effervescence (CO₂) and a brown precipitate of Fe(H₂O)₃(OH)₃.</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Write the equation for excess ammonia with [Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺.</summary><div><p>[Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺ + 4NH₃ → [Cu(NH₃)₄(H₂O)₂]²⁺ + 4H₂O, giving a deep blue solution.</p></div></details>\n","terms":[["Metal–aqua ion","A metal ion surrounded by six water ligands, [M(H₂O)₆]ⁿ⁺."],["Hydrolysis (of aqua ions)","A reaction in which a water ligand loses a proton to a water molecule, making the solution acidic."]],"qa":[["Cu²⁺(aq) + NaOH(aq)","Pale blue precipitate of Cu(OH)₂."],["Fe²⁺(aq) + NaOH(aq)","Green precipitate of Fe(OH)₂, turning orange-brown at the surface in air."],["Fe³⁺(aq) + NaOH(aq)","Orange-brown precipitate of Fe(OH)₃."],["Mn²⁺(aq) + NaOH(aq)","Light brown precipitate of Mn(OH)₂, darkening in air."],["Cr³⁺(aq) + NaOH(aq)","Grey-green precipitate of Cr(OH)₃; dissolves in excess to give dark green [Cr(OH)₆]³⁻."],["Cr³⁺(aq) + excess NH₃(aq)","Grey-green precipitate, dissolving to a purple solution of [Cr(NH₃)₆]³⁺."],["Which hydroxide precipitates dissolve in excess NaOH?","Only Cr(OH)₃ (amphoteric), of Cu, Fe, Mn and Cr."],["Test for ammonium ions","Warm with NaOH(aq): NH₃ turns damp red litmus blue."],["What colour is the precipitate from Fe²⁺ (aq) and NaOH?","Green, Fe(H₂O)₄(OH)₂; it turns brown on standing in air."],["What colour is the precipitate from Fe³⁺ (aq) and NaOH?","Brown (orange-brown), Fe(H₂O)₃(OH)₃."],["What happens when excess NaOH is added to the precipitate from Al³⁺?","It dissolves to form a colourless solution of [Al(OH)₄]⁻."],["What forms when Na₂CO₃ is added to Cu²⁺ (aq)?","A green-blue precipitate of CuCO₃ (no gas)."],["What forms when Na₂CO₃ is added to Al³⁺ (aq)?","Effervescence (CO₂) and a white precipitate of Al(H₂O)₃(OH)₃."],["What complex forms with excess concentrated HCl and Cu²⁺ (aq)?","Yellow-green [CuCl₄]²⁻, tetrahedral."]],"eqs":[],"mcq":[{"q":"Which aqua ion releases CO₂ when sodium carbonate is added?","o":["[Fe(H₂O)₆]²⁺","[Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺","[Fe(H₂O)₆]³⁺","[Co(H₂O)₆]²⁺"],"a":2,"e":"3+ aqua ions are acidic enough to release CO₂ from carbonate; 2+ ions form metal carbonate precipitates."},{"q":"Excess aqueous ammonia dissolves which precipitate?","o":["Fe(H₂O)₄(OH)₂","Cu(H₂O)₄(OH)₂","Fe(H₂O)₃(OH)₃","Al(H₂O)₃(OH)₃"],"a":1,"e":"Copper forms the deep blue [Cu(NH₃)₄(H₂O)₂]²⁺ complex."}],"exam":[],"labs":[],"pags":["rp11"],"packs":[{"set":"Topic questions","label":"Inorganic Chemistry 2 questions","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Topic-Qs/AQA/Inorganic-II/OUP-Chapterwide/Inorganic%20Chemistry%202%20QP.pdf"},{"set":"Topic questions","label":"Inorganic Chemistry 2 mark scheme","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Topic-Qs/AQA/Inorganic-II/OUP-Chapterwide/Inorganic%20Chemistry%202%20MS.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"PMT notes: Reaction of Metal Aqua Ions","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Inorganic-II/Detailed/2.6.%20Reaction%20of%20Metal%20Aqua%20Ions.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Knockhardy notes: Reactions of Transition Metal Ions - Cobalt, Copper, Iron, Manganese, Chromium, Silver and Vanadium","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Inorganic-II/Knockhardy/2.6.%20Reactions%20of%20Transition%20Metal%20Ions%20-%20Cobalt,%20Copper,%20Iron,%20Manganese,%20Chromium,%20Silver%20and%20Vanadium.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Mind map: Reactions of Ions in Aqueous Solution","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Inorganic-II/Mind-Maps/2.6.%20Reactions%20of%20Ions%20in%20Aqueous%20Solution.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Definitions: Inorganic Chemistry 2","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Inorganic-II/Definitions%20-%20Inorganic%20Chemistry%20II%20-%20AQA%20Chemistry%20A-level.pdf"},{"set":"Required practical guides","label":"RP 11: Identifying transition metal ions","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Practical-Skills/RP%2011%20-%20Identifying%20transition%20metal%20ions.pdf"}]});
})();
